//? Title: ResNeXt: Cardinality and Aggregated Transformations
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A grouped bottleneck represents multiple learned transformations with one efficient convolution.
We compare Form C against explicit split-transform-sum values and derivatives, check the complete
ImageNet architecture, then fit a compact 29-layer CIFAR model through a visible tensor Adam loop.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.3_residual_and_dense_families/resnext.cpp`.

> The method

Let C be cardinality, w the stage base and d=floor(w*base_width/64). A Form-C block reduces
input channels to Cd with a dense 1×1 filter, applies C independent d→d 3×3 convolutions,
then expands to 4w with a dense 1×1 filter. BN/ReLU follows the first two filters; final BN
precedes shortcut addition and ReLU. Later stage entries downsample in the grouped 3×3.
At unchanged input/output width 256, the branch kernel budget is

$$P=2(256)Cd+9Cd^2.$$
\alttext(Cardinality multiplies branch count; the grouped spatial term has no cross-group weights.)

The 1×64d and 32×4d choices use 69632 and 70144 branch kernel scalars, respectively.
Holding Cd fixed while increasing C reduces only the spatial-filter term. Cardinality
changes connectivity, so equal budgets do not imply equal functions or measured speed.
The separate transform/sum fixture slices the same weights, normalizes each reduced group,
and sums the final linear projections before the last BN; applying that BN separately to
branches would change the result. Nontrivial moving means/variances and gamma/beta expose
normalization mistakes. Both stride-one identity and stride-two projection cases compare
all input and parameter derivatives, with float32 relative tolerance 3e-5.

Full ResNeXt-50 32×4d uses a 7×7/2 stem and 3×3/2 pool, sixteen 3/4/6/3 blocks,
outer widths 256/512/1024/2048 and 56/28/14/7 feature sides at 224 pixels.
It has 25028904 learned and 68224 moving scalars. BN learns scale/shift with epsilon 1e-5.
He-uniform fan-in replaces the release's normal fan-out initialization; one grouped HWIO
leaf replaces per-group example filters. ImageNet depths 101/152 are also supported.

The compact fit preserves all nine CIFAR-29 bottlenecks: outer widths 64/128/256,
cardinality eight, d=4/8/16, stem width 16 and no initial pool. It has 359898 learned
and 6304 moving scalars. Ten epochs on 64 fitting images use batch eight, Adam(.001),
explicit .0001/2 kernel L2 and independent padded crop/flip augmentation. Thirty-two validation
images select paired parameter/BN checkpoints before evaluating 32 official-test images.
The original source's 12k-row/twelve-epoch run and the paper's long SGD run are different
experiments. This bounded fit illustrates optimization, not a published accuracy benchmark.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/resnext_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/resnext_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/resnext_concept.tikz > build/resnext_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/resnext_concept.svg build/resnext_concept.dvi > build/resnext_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
